========================================================================
  PART 2: THE PERPENDICULAR DIRECTION
========================================================================


  The n-2 embedding creates FIBERS over G_{n-2} classes.
  The PERPENDICULAR direction is variation WITHIN a fiber.

  Within a fiber, classes share the same inner tournament but
  differ in the SOURCE-SINK WIRING (boundary arcs).

  The wiring has 3 components:
  1. Source-to-middle arcs: n-2 binary choices
  2. Middle-to-sink arcs: n-2 binary choices
  3. Source-to-sink arc: 1 binary choice
  Total: 2(n-2) + 1 = 2n-3 wiring bits.

  The PERPENDICULAR GRAPH is the graph on each fiber induced
  by the arc-reversal edges of G_n.

  QUESTION: Is the perpendicular graph related to a HYPERCUBE?
  The wiring has 2n-3 bits, so the full wiring space is Q_{2n-3}.
  The perpendicular graph is a QUOTIENT of Q_{2n-3} by the
  automorphisms of the inner tournament.

  n=5: wiring bits = 7, Q_7 has 128 nodes.
  Fiber 0 has 9 classes (quotient of 128 by ~14)
  Fiber 1 has 8 classes (quotient of 128 by ~16)

  ARC TYPES at n=5:
  Total arcs: m = C(5,2) = 10
  Inner arcs: C(3,2) = 3 (arcs among middle 3 vertices)
  Boundary arcs: 10 - 3 = 7 (= 2n-3 = 7)
  Inner arc reversal: CHANGES the inner class (crosses fibers)
  Boundary arc reversal: STAYS in the same fiber (perpendicular)

  THEREFORE:
  Within-fiber edges correspond to BOUNDARY arc reversals (7 per vertex)
  Cross-fiber edges correspond to INNER arc reversals (3 per vertex)
  Total edges from each class: 10 = 7 boundary + 3 inner

  Class 0 (H=1, fiber 0): deg=6, within=5, cross=1
  Class 1 (H=3, fiber 0): deg=3, within=3, cross=0
  Class 2 (H=5, fiber 0): deg=7, within=5, cross=2
  Class 2 (H=5, fiber 1): deg=7, within=4, cross=3
  Class 3 (H=3, fiber 1): deg=4, within=3, cross=1
  Class 4 (H=9, fiber 1): deg=6, within=5, cross=1

========================================================================
  PART 3: IS G_5 A PRODUCT OF G_3 AND A FIBER GRAPH?
========================================================================


  If G_5 were a PRODUCT graph G_3 x F (where F is the fiber graph):
  - |V(G_5)| = |V(G_3)| * |V(F)|
  - |E(G_5)| = |V(G_3)| * |E(F)| + |V(F)| * |E(G_3)|
  - Each (inner, wiring) pair gives one G_5 class

  CHECKING:
  |V(G_5)| = 12. |V(G_3)| = 2.
  If product: |V(F)| = 12/2 = 6. But fibers have sizes 9 and 8!
  So G_5 is NOT a simple product (fibers have different sizes).

  Instead: G_5 is a FIBER BUNDLE over G_3 with varying fiber size.
  Fiber over class 0 (H=1): 9 classes
  Fiber over class 1 (H=3): 8 classes
  Total: 9 + 8 = 17. But |V(G_5)| = 12!
  So: fibers OVERLAP. 17 - 12 = 5 classes are in BOTH fibers.

  Fiber 0 (inner H=1): 9 classes
  Fiber 1 (inner H=3): 8 classes
  Overlap: 5 classes (in BOTH fibers)
  Only in fiber 0: 4 classes
  Only in fiber 1: 3 classes

  OVERLAP CLASSES (multiple inner types):
    Class 2 (H=5, scores=(1, 1, 2, 2, 4)): inner classes = [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    Class 4 (H=9, scores=(1, 1, 2, 3, 3)): inner classes = [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    Class 5 (H=5, scores=(0, 2, 2, 3, 3)): inner classes = [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    Class 6 (H=9, scores=(1, 1, 2, 3, 3)): inner classes = [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    Class 11 (H=15, scores=(2, 2, 2, 2, 2)): inner classes = [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])

  INTERPRETATION:
  The overlap classes are those where DIFFERENT source-sink choices
  reveal DIFFERENT inner tournaments. These are the classes at the
  "intersection" of two fibers.

  In a fiber bundle, the OVERLAP is the "transition function"
  between two local trivializations. The transition function tells
  you how to glue the fibers together.


========================================================================
  PART 4: CROSS-FIBER EDGES = INNER ARC REVERSALS
========================================================================

  Cross-fiber edges change the inner class (inner arc reversal).

  Pure cross-fiber edges (only_0 <-> only_1): 3
  Edges involving overlap classes: 29

  G_3 has 1 edge (transitive <-> 3-cycle).
  This edge 'lifts' to 3 cross-fiber edges in G_5.
  Lift factor: 3

